{-
   What `@convention` and `@host` refuse - the two attributes this stage parsed and threw away until
   2026-09-07.

   That is the failure worth a fixture rather than the spellings: a convention silently dropped is a
   body entered under one its author did not write, and a `@host` field silently dropped is a record
   whose JS shape nobody has said anything about. So the subject here is that each is *read*, and the
   refusals are what reading them looks like.

   The messages are the old shipping stage's, word for word - one language, one diagnostic - and
   `../Reject.VectorReset.yana` and `../Reject.Host.yana` are the same programs. This was a fixture
   of its own rather than a directive on those two, because a rejection fixture names one resolver
   and those two were the shipping stage's. The one rule that genuinely differed is placement: that
   stage read a convention off a plain `fn` only, and this one reads it off every signature, beside
   the encoding marker `readInlineAttribute` already reads there.
-}

-- Two of the six conventions the lower IR has are the compiler's own choice between internal shapes,
-- and one describes what a syscall leaves alone rather than how anything is entered. Naming one is
-- writing an implementation detail down as though it were an interface.
@convention(simple)
fn internalShape(x: Int) -> Int = x

@convention(system)
fn kernelSide(x: Int) -> Int = x

-- A name no convention has.
@convention(fastcall)
fn unknownName(x: Int) -> Int = x

-- Exactly one argument, and it is a name rather than an expression.
@convention
fn noArgument(x: Int) -> Int = x

@convention(clobber, sysv)
fn twoArguments(x: Int) -> Int = x

-- Two of them on one declaration say two different things about how one function is entered.
@convention(clobber)
@convention(sysv)
fn twice(x: Int) -> Int = x

-- And where it means nothing at all: an attribute about a call boundary, written on a type.
@convention(clobber)
data Placed {n: Int}

-- `@host` names a property of the value the record holds, so there has to be a value for it to be a
-- property *of*, and the field that holds that value cannot itself be one of the properties.
data Alone {length: @host Int}
data First {length: @host Int, items: Int}

-- One stored field, not two. A record with two of its own has an object either way, and there is
-- nothing for the elided field to be reached through.
data Two {items: Int, count: Int, length: @host Int}

-- `@bits` narrows a field into a word it shares with its neighbours and an elided field has no word;
-- `@box` makes a field an address and an elided one has no storage to take the address of.
data Narrow {items: Int, length: @host @bits(30) Int}
data Boxed {items: Int, length: @host @box Int}

-- The property is the field's own name, so there is nothing to pass.
data Argued {items: Int, length: @host(size) Int}

-- And outside a field, where it says nothing about the type it was written on.
fn elided(n: @host Int) -> Int = n

fn main() -> Int = 0
